  "body": "### Summary\n\n`SessionRefreshFlow.adoptRefresh` can write the `hash_check` from a refresh made with the old cookies over the token of a same-account re-login that landed while the refresh ran. The next POST then gets HFR's bad-token answer (W17b). The case is rare but cheap to fence. Found by CodeRabbit on #309 (outside-diff finding at b2e83ff, steward analysis on #309); the operator asked for this follow-up on 2026-10-29.\n\n### Where\n\n`Packages/HFRKit/Sources/HFRClient/Session/RefreshUpdate.swift:53-73` (`adoptRefresh`).\n- **Cookies are already safe.** Each refreshed cookie is written only if that cookie key still holds the seed's value, and a re-login rewrites `md_passs`/`md_user`.\n- **The token is written when `hashCheck == seed.hashCheck`.** Identity is checked by `userKey(now:)`, so a re-login of the **same** account passes the identity check.\n\nThe race needs all three of these:\n1. a same-account re-login lands while a refresh is in flight;\n2. the login's token equals the seeded token (H0);\n3. the refresh, made with the old cookies, returns a different token (H1).\n\nThe result is re-login cookies paired with token H1. Nothing is sent under another account and no data is lost: the next POST is refused, and the next form load records a fresh token.\n\n### Scope\n\n- Add a login generation to `HFRSession` (HFRNetwork):\n  - `public private(set) var loginGeneration: Int`;\n  - `public func advanceLoginGeneration()`, called by LOT-043's `adoptLogin` after it installs the cookies and token (`HFRClient/Login/HFRSession+Login.swift`).\n- Carry the generation in `RefreshSeed`. `adoptRefresh` returns `.unconfirmed(.sessionChanged)` and writes nothing when the generation moved.\n- Coordinate with #308 / PR #315 (`HashCheckSeed`, compare-and-set in `PostingFlow`/`TopicFlagFlow`). If #315 is merged first, consider having `HashCheckSeed` use the same generation, so one fence covers every token write. Otherwise keep the two independent and say so in the LOT-043/044 specs.\n- This is a public API addition to HFRNetwork (`HFRSession`), so it needs no ADR. Sessions and tokens are a risky flow: the steward or core merges it, and an outside contributor's PR needs a cross-contributor review (EXTERNAL-AGENTS §7).\n\n### Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] A test on a real `HFRSession`, with no network:\n  - seed a refresh;\n  - `adoptLogin` the same account with the same token;\n  - adopt a refresh update carrying a different token;\n  - expect `.unconfirmed(.sessionChanged)`, with the login's cookies and token kept.\n\n  The test is shown to fail without the generation check.\n- [ ] The existing LOT-044 tests are unchanged and green (`SessionRefreshFlowTests`, the `CapturedSessionCheckTests` in the App).\n- [ ] The LOT-044 spec edge cases and the LOT-043 spec describe the fence; the HFR-PROTOCOL LOT-044 subsection needs no change (no new HFR fact).\n- [ ] `swift test` green on Linux and macOS CI; `scripts/check-lot-deps.py` passes.\n\n### Model\n\nOpus → Fable (sessions and tokens).\n\n### Sources\n\n- The CodeRabbit finding and the steward's trace are on PR #309.\n- LOT-043 and LOT-044 specs.\n- `docs/agents/LESSONS.md` row 54 (compare-and-set on token writes, #308).",
